              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Fig. 9.12: Gas exchange through alveoli"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.563,
            0.084,
            0.883,
            0.122
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "How does the exchange of gases happen?"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.562,
            0.125,
            0.884,
            0.328
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Through the process of breathing, fresh air from outside enters the lungs via specific pathway and fills the alveoli. The alveoli have thin walls surrounded by fine tubes containing blood (Fig. 9.12). Blood carries carbon dioxide from the body to the alveoli, where it is released into the air. At the same time, oxygen from the alveoli passes into the"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.253,
            0.327,
            0.728,
            0.347
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "blood and is transported to all parts of the body."
            }
          ],
          "continues_prev": true
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.253,
            0.349,
            0.884,
            0.461
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Have you ever wondered how the food you eat gives you energy? The key is not only the food but also the oxygen we breathe! When we eat food, our body breaks it down into simple substances like sugar (glucose). Oxygen helps break down glucose to release energy. This process is called respiration. The word equation of the process of "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "respiration",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " is as follows—"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.31,
            0.467,
            0.829,
            0.486
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Glucose + Oxygen Carbon dioxide + Water + Energy"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.179,
            0.483,
            0.633,
            0.682
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 8,
              "bbox": [
                0.179,
                0.483,
                0.633,
                0.682
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_11_image_8.jpg",
              "content": ""
            },
            {
              "type": "image_caption",
              "index": 9,
              "bbox": [
                0.222,
                0.689,
                0.598,
                0.721
              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Fig. 9.13: The percentage of oxygen and carbon dioxide in inhaled and exhaled air"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.643,
            0.49,
            0.884,
            0.73
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "During breathing, we inhale air from our surroundings and exhale air having more carbon dioxide than the inhaled air. Note that not all the oxygen is used up (Fig. 9.13). Some other animals can use a larger fraction of the oxygen during respiration. This exchange of gases"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.251,
            0.73,
            0.883,
            0.823
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "ensures that each segment of our body gets oxygen to produce energy and remove waste products. In simple words, breathing brings in oxygen and removes carbon dioxide, while respiration uses oxygen to break down food and release energy. This energy helps us walk, run, play, and even think!"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.253,
            0.824,
            0.883,
            0.881
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Breathing is a physical process, while respiration is a chemical process that occurs inside the body. Both the processes are essential for our survival!"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.004,
            0.919,
            0.27,
            0.985
          ],
          "content": [
